<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tigurini</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tigurini were one of the four cantons (pagi) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a>, a Celtic people of the Swiss plateau. Joining the migration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a>, they defeated and killed the consul Lucius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-longinus">Cassius Longinus</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agen">Agen</a> in 107 BCE under their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divico">Divico</a>. In 58 BCE, during the Helvetian migration, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> destroyed them at the crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Arar</a> (Saône).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tigurini.jpg" alt="Tigurini" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Gleyre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Gleyre_Les_Romans_p.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name has often been derived from the Celtic word for lord, *tigerno-, as in Old Irish tigern and Welsh teyrn, though Peter Schrijver found this doubtful and Xavier Delamarre proposed instead the Celtic tigu-, last or final. Ancient writers name the Tigurini only in connection with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbrian-war">Cimbrian War</a> and Caesar's campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Caesar's statement that they formed one of four pagi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> is the usual basis for placing them among that people, but Appian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> keep the two apart, and Michel Tarpin has argued that Caesar thereby extended to all the Helvetii a grievance that concerned the Tigurini alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a>, quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, the plunder of the passing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> drew the Tigurini and the otherwise unknown Tougeni away to join them. The Tigurini seem to have shared in the defeat of the consul Marcus Junius Silanus in 109 BCE, and in 108 BCE they supported the revolt of the Tectosages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agen">Agen</a> the legate Lucius Piso fell with Cassius, and the survivors were sent under the yoke; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> remembered the defeat as the clades Cassiana. After the Cimbri and Teutoni were destroyed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aquae Sextiae</a> and Vercellae, the Tigurini, posted as a reserve in the Noric Alps according to Florus, scattered and escaped.</p><p>Nothing is heard of them for four decades; a team led by Thierry Luginbühl has proposed that they moved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> valley to the western Swiss Plateau. Caesar presented his blow of 58 BCE as late revenge for the clades Cassiana, Piso being the grandfather of his father-in-law, and named the Helvetian envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divico">Divico</a> as the victor of 107 BCE, though Gerold Walser doubts they were one man. Under the Empire an inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aventicum">Avenches</a> records a pagus Tigurinus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divico">Divico</a> and the victory of 107 BCE were credited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> as a whole, ancestors for the federal state founded in 1848, as in Charles Gleyre's painting The Romans passing under the yoke of 1858. In the early 16th century humanists gave Zurich the Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name Tigurum, used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> and the Froschauer press; a Roman tombstone found on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindenhof">Lindenhof</a> in 1747 showed the Roman name to have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turicum">Turicum</a>, and Tigurum was abandoned by the end of the 18th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tigurini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were divided into four cantons, and one of them, the <b>Tigurini</b> under Divico, joined the Cimbri and defeated a Roman army near Agen in 107 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetii">Helvetii</a></li><li><blockquote>In 107 BCE they rebelled with the help of the migrating Cimbri and <b>Tigurini</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/volcae-tectosages">Volcae Tectosages</a></li></ul></section>
